description:
Implement `IntVector`, a growable array of `int`, from the reference below. It follows `std::vector` closely, but its pages pin down details the standard leaves open, such as exactly how capacity grows, so the tests can check them. Manage the memory yourself (`std::unique_ptr<int[]>` or `new[]`/`delete[]`); do not use `std::vector` inside it.

```cpp
class IntVector
{
public:
    static constexpr std::size_t maxSize() { return std::size_t{ 1 } << 28; }

    IntVector();
    explicit IntVector(std::size_t count, int value = 0);
    IntVector(std::initializer_list<int> values);
    IntVector(const IntVector& other);
    IntVector(IntVector&& other) noexcept;
    IntVector& operator=(const IntVector& other);
    IntVector& operator=(IntVector&& other) noexcept;

    std::size_t size() const;
    std::size_t capacity() const;
    bool empty() const;
    int* data();

    int& operator[](std::size_t i);
    int& at(std::size_t i);
    int& front();
    int& back();

    void push_back(int value);
    void pop_back();
    std::size_t insert(std::size_t pos, int value);
    std::size_t insert(std::size_t pos, std::size_t count, int value);
    std::size_t erase(std::size_t pos);
    std::size_t erase(std::size_t first, std::size_t last);
    void resize(std::size_t n, int value = 0);
    void reserve(std::size_t n);
    void shrinkToFit();
    void clear();

    bool operator==(const IntVector& other) const;
};
```

**Construction.** The default vector is empty with capacity 0. `IntVector(count, value)` holds `count` copies of `value`; the initializer-list constructor holds the listed values (so `IntVector{ 3 }` holds one element, 3, while `IntVector(3)` holds three zeros). In all three, capacity equals size. A **copy** has the same elements and capacity equal to its size; copies are independent. After a **move**, the target has the source's elements and capacity, and the source is empty with capacity 0.

**Access.** `operator[]`, `front`, `back` and `pop_back` do not check: calling them out of range or on an empty vector is undefined behaviour (never tested). `at` **throws `std::out_of_range`** if `i >= size()`. `data` returns a pointer to the first element.

**Growth.** A **reallocation** moves the elements to a new block of a given capacity. Reallocation happens only where stated below; otherwise pointers from `data()` stay valid.

| Member | Behaviour | Capacity |
|---|---|---|
| `push_back` | appends | if full, becomes `max(1, 2 × capacity)` |
| `insert(pos, value)` | inserts before index `pos`; returns `pos` | if full, as `push_back` |
| `insert(pos, count, value)` | inserts `count` copies before `pos`; returns `pos`; `count` 0 changes nothing | if `size + count > capacity`, becomes `max(2 × capacity, size + count)` |
| `erase(pos)` | removes element `pos`; returns `pos` | unchanged |
| `erase(first, last)` | removes `[first, last)`; returns `first` | unchanged |
| `resize(n, value)` | shrinks to `n`, or grows filling with `value` | if `n > capacity`, becomes `n` |
| `reserve(n)` | nothing else | if `n > capacity`, becomes `n`; never shrinks |
| `shrinkToFit` | nothing else | becomes `size` |
| `clear` | removes all elements | unchanged |

**Errors.** Both `insert`s throw `std::out_of_range` if `pos > size()`. `erase(pos)` throws `std::out_of_range` if `pos >= size()`; `erase(first, last)` if `first > last` or `last > size()`. `reserve` throws `std::length_error` if `n > maxSize()`. In every case the vector is unchanged when they throw.

**Comparison.** Two vectors are equal if they have the same size and the same elements in order; capacity does not matter.

